  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A telescope heard something once and never again. August 1977, Ohio. Seventy-two seconds of narrow radio noise from Sagittarius.",
        "visual_query": "radio telescope dish night",
        "visual_fallback": "night sky stars"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Jerry Ehman found it on the printout days later. He circled the letters and wrote one word in the margin: Wow.",
        "visual_query": "printed computer paper readout",
        "visual_fallback": "old paper printout"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It sat almost exactly on the hydrogen line, 1420 megahertz. That frequency is protected, and it is where you would listen for someone.",
        "visual_query": "hydrogen spectrum frequency graphic",
        "visual_fallback": "science diagram"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Seventy-two seconds is exactly how long the beam took to sweep that patch of sky, so the source was out there, not on Earth.",
        "visual_query": "observatory antenna sky sweep",
        "visual_fallback": "telescope silhouette"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Dozens of searches since have found nothing there. A 2017 comet explanation was proposed, and other astronomers took it apart.",
        "visual_query": "big ear ohio observatory",
        "visual_fallback": "empty radio dish"
      }
    ],
